-- title:
--   A generator of a height-one prime of K[X] has order 1
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, and let $w$ be a point of the height-one spectrum of the polynomial ring $K[X]$, i.e. a nonzero prime ideal `w.asIdeal` of height one. Let $p \in K[X]$ be nonzero and assume that `w.asIdeal` is the principal ideal generated by $p$. Associated with $w$ is the place `Place.ofHeightOneSpectrum w` of the rational function field $\mathrm{RatFunc}(K)$ over $K$: the valuation subring of the $w$-adic valuation of $\mathrm{RatFunc}(K)$, packaged as a `Place`, that is, a valuation subring of $\mathrm{RatFunc}(K)$ containing the image of $K$, different from the whole field, and a principal ideal ring. For a place $v$, $v.\mathrm{ord}(f)$ is defined as minus the `WithZero.log` of the value at $f$ of the adic valuation attached to $v$, valued in $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$, so that $\mathrm{ord}$ is the additive normalisation of the valuation with $\mathrm{ord}$ of a uniformiser equal to $1$. The assertion is that the order of the image of $p$ under the structure map $K[X] \to \mathrm{RatFunc}(K)$ at this place equals $1$; equivalently, any generator of $w$ is a uniformiser at the corresponding finite place of $K(t)/K$.
--
--   This is the basic normalisation statement for the finite places of the rational function field: the finite places of $K(t)$ correspond to the height-one primes of $K[X]$, and a generator of such a prime has order exactly one there. It is used throughout the treatment of divisors on $\mathbb{P}^1$, for instance in computing degrees of principal divisors and in evaluating functions at places, including at the place at infinity.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_RationalFunctionField_ord_ofHeightOneSpectrum_of_span.lean

